   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT in relation to the designation of the East Hampton Volunteer Ocean\nRescue and Auxiliary Squad as an emergency rescue and first aid squad pursuant\nto the general municipal law and permitting members to display green lights on\nmotor vehicles when engaged in emergency operation pursuant to the vehicle and\ntraffic law",
    "basePrintNo": "S5571",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5571-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Suffolk County",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5571\n\nSPONSOR: LAVALLE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act in relation to the designation of the East\nHampton Volunteer Ocean Rescue and Auxiliary Squad as an emergency\nrescue and first aid squad pursuant to the general municipal law and\npermitting members to display green lights on motor vehicles when\nengaged in emergency operation pursuant to the vehicle and traffic law\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nDesignates the East Hampton Volunteer Ocean Rescue and Auxiliary Squad\n(EHVOR) as an emergency rescue and first aid squad under Section 209-b\nof the General Municipal Law.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Authorizes the Town of East Hampton to designate EHVOR, by\nthe adoption of a resolution by the Town Board, as an emergency rescue\nand first aid squad under Section 209-b of the General Municipal Law\n\nSection 2. Once officially designated an emergency rescue and first aid\nsquad under Section 209-b of the General Municipal Law by the East Hamp-\nton Town Board, members of EHVOR may display a green light on motor\nvehicles pursuant to paragraph 5 of subdivision 41 of Section 375 of the\nVehicle and Traffic Law.\n\nSection 3. Immediate effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nEHVOR has been responding to water emergencies, 24 hours a day, 365 days\na year, since 2003. Their mission is to make expedient and safe water\nrescues year-round at all unprotected beaches in East Hampton Town.\n\nOver the last few years, the leadership of EHVOR has worked very hard to\norganize and structure the squad in a manner similar to Fire Depart-\nments. They have created and adopted Standard Operating Procedures\n(SOPs), Bylaws, as well as an updated Memo of Understanding with the\nTown of East Hampton and the Village of East Hampton. The Chain of\nCommand consists of Chiefs, Captains and Lieutenants with three squads\nout of Montauk, Amagansett and East Hampton Village.  Each of these\nsquads are set up with Jet ski response vehicles for year-round service\nand all of the Suffolk County Volunteer Lifeguards that serve as members\nof EHVOR are certified in (NIMS) National Incident Management System so\nthat they are able to respond to multi-agency calls like the plane crash\nin Quogue last year where members responded to and supported operations\nhand-in-hand with the Coast Guard and other emergency organizations.\nThey train year-round for all types of water emergencies and have start-\ned to expand training/expertise to other advance water rescues like\nswift water rescues for flood situations.\n\nThis legislation is a special act for the State of New York to recognize\nthe critical life-saving functions and services carried out by EHVOR by\nauthorizing the Town of East Hampton to designate EHVOR, by the adoption\nof a resolution by the Town Board, as an emergency rescue and first aid\nsquad under Section 209-b of the General Municipal Law. Once designated,\none green light may be affixed to any motor vehicle owned by an EHVOR\nmember, a family member or a business enterprise in which such member\nhas a proprietary interest or by which is employed, provided such member\nhas been authorized in writing to so affix a green light by the chief\nofficer. Such green light may only be displayed when the member is\nengaged in an emergency operation.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019: New Legislation\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
